# Residual Learning and Residual Networks

This architecture is designed to enable efficient training and introduces the concept of skip connection

Overall this can help
 - Prevent vanishing gradient
 - Info pass through cleanly
 - build bigger and more complex networks


## Why Residual Networks over Deep NN

- Vanishing/Exploding Gradient: In deep NN, especially in large NNs gradients can become very very tiny or very very big during backprop, which can make training unstable
- Degradation: increasing a network may degrade its performance
    - Performance plateau
    - Accuracy Degradation

## Architecture

- Residual connections
    - Identity Shortcuts
    - Reduces vanishing gradient 
- Identity mapping
    - Learn residual mappings instead of full mappings
- Depth
    - Extremely deep architectures can perform well in ResNets
- Fewer parameters:
    - High accuracy with few params

### ResNet-34

> -First stage: 3 residual blocks, each with 2 convolution layers of 64 filters and identity skip connections
> -Second stage: 4 residual blocks, each with 2 convolution layers of 128 filters; uses 1×1 projection or padding for dimension matching
> -Third stage: 6 residual blocks, each with 2 convolution layers of 256 filters
> -Fourth stage: 3 residual blocks, each with 2 convolution layers of 512 filters
Output layer: Feature maps are passed through Global Average Pooling followed by a fully connected layer with softmax for classification


## How it works

Instead of learning the full representaation like a CNN, resnets learn a "residual function" which makes optimization faster and easier

In normal nueral networks by the time we finish training there is a lot of noise and randomness that once we backprop the gradient vanishes or explodes

So our goal becomes: **Orginal data to be towards the later layers and the gradients should arrive in the earlier networks for them to improve**


### Residual Block

Data can be passed through both through and around using skip connections

We can add or concatenate the input and output of the block itself. 
In the image below it shows the input as the "X identity" which is just the output of the previous block

